    <h2>1. The Code Mandate: IRC Section R312.1.3 Opening Limitations</h2>
    <p>
      The International Residential Code specifies two distinct opening rules that apply to interior and exterior staircases:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The Primary 4-Inch Rule:</strong> <em>"Required guards shall not have openings from the walking surface to the required guard height that allow passage of a sphere 4 inches (102 mm) in diameter."</em> This applies to the clear space between vertical balusters, spindles, cables, and horizontal rails.</li>
      <li><strong>The 6-Inch Triangle Exception:</strong> <em>"The triangular openings formed by the riser, tread, and bottom rail of a guard at the open side of a stairway shall not allow passage of a sphere 6 inches (152 mm) in diameter."</em> Because the diagonal bottom rail rises above the corner of each tread, building code permits a slightly larger 6-inch triangular clearance before balusters must terminate.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2. Clear Opening vs. On-Center Spacing Math</h2>
    <p>
      Building codes dictate the <strong>clear distance</strong> between adjacent balusters, NOT the distance between their centers. If you layout balusters at 4 inches on-center, a 1/2-inch square iron baluster leaves 3.5 inches of clear space—which easily passes inspection.
    </p>
    <p>
      However, when homeowners assume they can install "2 balusters per tread" without checking their tread run, failure is common:
    </p>
    <div class="bg-amber-50 border-l-4 border-amber-600 p-4 font-mono text-sm text-amber-900 my-4">
      Example: 11-Inch Tread Run with Two 1/2-Inch Iron Balusters<br/>
      • Total Tread Run = 11.0 Inches<br/>
      • Two balusters divide the tread into 2 equal spaces: 11.0 / 2 = 5.5 inches center-to-center<br/>
      • Clear Opening Gap = 5.5 inches - 0.50 inch baluster thickness = 5.00 INCHES!<br/>
      • Result: FAILS CODE (A 4-inch sphere passes through with 1 inch of extra clearance!)
    </div>
    <p>
      For any tread with a run exceeding 9.5 inches when using 1/2-inch iron balusters, you <strong>must install 3 balusters per tread</strong> or use a shoe base with wider intermediate spacers.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Baluster Spacing Reference Table (1/2" Square Iron Spindles)</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6 not-prose">
      <table class="w-full text-left text-xs border border-gray-200 rounded-lg">
