    <h2>4. How Building Officials Inspect Railings in the Field</h2>
    <p>
      When an inspector from the city building department arrives for your final framing or occupancy inspection, they carry a physical <strong>4-inch precision wooden or plastic inspection sphere</strong>. They will roll this sphere down every single baluster opening, pressing it against the top, middle, and bottom of the rail.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the sphere passes through or wedges with the equator crossing the center plane, the guardrail fails immediately. Additionally, the inspector will shake the newel post and handrail forcefully to test for 200 lbs of concentrated load deflection. Any perceptible wobble or deflection exceeding 1/2 inch will result in a reinspection fee.
    </p>
  </div>

