    note="While the 4-inch sphere rule is universal across all jurisdictions adhering to the IRC, horizontal cable railings carry an additional inspection hurdle known as the 'ladder effect.' Between 2000 and 2009, the IRC banned horizontal members on guards that could facilitate climbing by toddlers. While the national model code later removed that specific climbing ban, DOZENS of local city and county building departments (especially in California, Florida, and parts of the Midwest) maintain local amendments prohibiting horizontal cables on guards over 30 inches high! If you are considering horizontal cable instead of vertical balusters, check with your local municipal building official before spending thousands on stainless cable swaging kits."
